      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(control-ui): agent identity editor, Settings-homed Agents page, scalable chip switcher",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nResolves a set of Control UI problems around agent identity and navigation:\n\n- Agents with no configured identity show their raw id (for example `main`) in the sidebar chip and chat, and there is no way to set an agent's display name, emoji, or avatar image from the web UI at all. The identity plumbing (`agents.update`, identity config, workspace `IDENTITY.md`, avatar serving) already exists server-side but is not exposed.\n- The Agents management page lives in the customizable sidebar nav (\"More\" menu) even though it is configuration, while Settings already has an \"AI & Agents\" group. Settings placement was inconsistent.\n- The sidebar agent-chip switcher renders every agent as a flat list, which does not scale past a handful of agents, and there is no way to reach agent management from the chip.\n- Switching that chip scopes only Chat and the sidebar session list; Usage, Automations, Tasks, Workboard, and Sessions remain global, which makes the active-agent context misleading.\n- Sidebar chrome (nav labels, session rows, the chip) is text-selectable, so drag interactions select label text and read as broken.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe identity editor writes through the existing `agents.update` RPC, which persists to `agents.list[].identity` and rewrites the workspace `IDENTITY.md` — so the agent itself and the UI share one identity source, and an agent can still change its own name/emoji/avatar by editing that file. Avatar uploads are downscaled client-side (256px WebP/PNG data URL) so the persisted config stays small and within the existing 2 MB avatar policy.\n\nThe Agents page moves under Settings (`/settings/agents`, old `/agents` kept as an alias) into the existing Agents group, following the same `definePage` alias pattern as other settings slices. The chip menu becomes the primary switcher: above ten agents it shows pinned agents first plus an inline filter over the full roster; pins persist like the existing sidebar nav pins (browser-profile scoped). A new \"Agent settings\" row deep-links to the selected agent via `?agent=`.\n\nAgent selection now separates the concrete chat agent (`selectedId`) from the shared page scope (`scopeId`). A chip switch updates both; each agent-owned page can widen the shared scope to All agents without losing the concrete chat agent. Explicit session deep links still open their target, and Agents settings keeps its independent `?agent=` selection. Cron summaries, jobs, and run history use an additive server-side agent filter so their counts and rows agree.\n\n## User Impact\n\n- Agents page Overview tab gains an Identity card: display name, emoji, and avatar image upload with live preview; saving updates the sidebar chip, chat, and `IDENTITY.md`.\n- Agents now lives in Settings → Agents; `/agents` URLs still work. Sidebar \"More\" menu no longer lists it.\n- Agent chip menu: \"Agent settings\" row; rosters >10 agents get pinned-first rows and a find-agent filter. Pin/unpin from the Agents page toolbar.\n- Chip switches scope Usage, Automations, Tasks, Workboard, and Sessions as well as Chat. Every scoped page has an Agent selector with an All agents escape.\n- Direct session links keep working across scope changes; choosing All agents does not change the active chat agent; Agents settings remains independent.\n- Sidebar text is no longer selectable (inputs inside menus still are).\n\n## Evidence\n\n- New/updated jsdom tests: `ui/src/components/app-sidebar.test.ts` (agent-settings navigation row, ≤10 plain roster, pinned+filter behavior, no-pin fallback), `ui/src/app/settings.node.test.ts` (pinned-agent persistence + normalization), `ui/src/app-navigation.test.ts` (route table + settings groups).\n- Live verification on a production gateway (source checkout): setting `identity.name`/`identity.emoji` via the same config path immediately renders the sidebar chip as the configured name with emoji avatar (gateway hot-reloads config; no restart).\n- Agent-scope regression coverage: focused UI suite 260/260; Gateway Cron 44, protocol 22, run-log 19; final affected UI rerun 34/34.\n- Browser E2E verifies chip → scoped Usage → All agents → independent Agents settings, including the stale route-loader race.\n- Source-blind behavior validation satisfies the contract (0.96 confidence, no findings); fresh autoreview reports no accepted/actionable findings.\n- Blacksmith delegated Testbox `pnpm check:changed` passed on the exact code patch in 6m24s.\n- Docs updated: `docs/web/control-ui.md` covers the footer chip/switcher, shared page scope, All agents, deep links, Settings → Agents, and identity editing.\n",
